-- `Bash(find:* -exec*)`, `-execdir`, `-delete`, `-ok`, `-okdir`, `-fprint*` —
+- `Bash(find *-exec*)`, `-execdir`, `-delete`, `-ok`, `-okdir`, `-fprint*` —
   `find` only allows read-only traversal forms; `-exec` is otherwise
   effectively shell escape.
+
+  The spelling matters and used to be wrong. These were written
+  `Bash(find:* -exec*)`, which Claude Code rejects — "the `:*` pattern must
+  be at the end" — and a rejected rule is **skipped**, so `find -exec` was
+  never denied at all. `:*` means prefix-match and may only terminate the
+  pattern; anything else with a `*` in it is a wildcard match against the
+  whole command. `find *-exec*` is that, and it catches `find . -exec …` and
+  the path-less `find -exec …` alike while leaving `find . -name …` allowed.
+  All three measured against Claude Code 2.1.269.
 - `Bash(git config core.hooksPath:*)` — no redirecting commit hooks to
   attacker-controlled paths.
 - `sandbox.enabled: false` — nono is the OS boundary; Claude Code's softer
   in-process sandbox would add friction without restricting an already
   sandboxed session.
 
